Chapter 10, Part 31: "I'm Home, Welcome Back"



—Come to think of it, Flügel was also a figure shrouded in mystery.


The first time Subaru heard that name was, well, probably when he obtained information about the "Great Tree of Flügel." He had been told that Flügel was a great man who had left no notable achievements other than planting that enormous tree, and Subaru's impression had been merely that leaving one's name in history was impressive enough in itself.

After that, since they had ended up cutting down the problematic Great Tree in order to defeat the White Whale, Flügel could also be called a benefactor to whom he owed both an apology and a thank-you for planting the tree.


But the next time he heard Flügel's name, things started to smell distinctly fishy. It was when Shaula—the Star Keeper of the Pleiades Watchtower—spoke of him as her master.

Shaula had originally been hailed as one of Lugunica's Three Great Heroes, known as the "Sage," but it turned out that her achievements had actually been pushed onto her by Flügel. On top of that, Shaula had, for some reason, persistently insisted that Subaru was that master of hers. —In the end, the truth of that matter remained unresolved, and Subaru's feelings toward Flügel were complicated.


Flügel could be called a benefactor who gave the final push in the White Whale subjugation battle, but he could also be called an unbelievably heartless person who trapped Shaula in the Watchtower for an eternity. If Subaru were allowed to be completely selfish about it, he'd want to claim that if Flügel was like that, then the title of Shaula's master should belong to him instead.


"Man, I sound so wishy-washy even to myself. ...But when you hear 'Flügel' right after hearing 'Kugelschreiber Castle,' it starts to feel like it's paying homage to German again..."


If Kugelschreiber meant ballpoint pen in German, then Flügel should mean wing in the same language. There had been a period when Subaru had desperately sought out cool-sounding words and arrived at German. You could call it a classic adolescent phase.


"Like Staubsauger—vacuum cleaner—or Zitronensaft—lemon juice. And not to hide anything, but the 'Schwarz' in Natsumi Schwarz also means 'black' in German."


"Even if you say so, I have no idea what you're talking about. ...So what you're saying is, Betty's family home and Subaru's hometown use the same words?"


"That's right. Well, since I suspect that 'Hoshin of the Wasteland' also came from the same hometown as me, it's not that surprising that those kinds of words are scattered around."


"Even Betty doesn't know how the castle got its name. But if it's Mother, Betty wouldn't find it strange no matter who she was acquainted with."


At Beatrice's words as she was being held, Subaru shifted his grip on her and let out a heavy sigh with a troubled expression, saying, "Yeah, I guess so."

Indeed, at the tea party in the "Sanctuary," Echidna—the young Echidna, that is—had said something about people from all over the world seeking knowledge and gathering to meet her. At that moment, since he hadn't had anything particular to ask Echidna, he had let it slide, but now he felt like he had missed a precious opportunity.


"Well, how much of what young Echidna says can be trusted is questionable, but she did seem to have pride in showing off her knowledge. Maybe I should have asked her about the truth of the world?"


"What would Subaru even do if he learned the truth of the world? In the first place, Betty doesn't think the world is lying about anything."


"Well, yeah, I guess."


He had said it without much thought, but indeed, even if he knew the truth of the world, he'd probably have trouble handling it.

At present, what he wanted was the safety of his companions and a way to contact them, a means to save the kingdom, and a way to keep the enemy away forever—something along those lines. The truth of the world certainly had no role to play there.

At any rate—


"—Who would've thought the trip to Flügel's Great Tree would get approved so easily."


As Subaru muttered that, what he saw in the distance, standing alone, was the aforementioned Great Tree of Flügel—or rather, its enormous, enormous stump.

The trunk portion, cut down for the White Whale subjugation, had been hauled away afterward and, being the waste-free resource it was, had apparently been used as spiritually potent timber for the kingdom. Most likely, it was now serving as buildings made from the Great Tree of Flügel, active in various places.

The remaining stump had become a valuable tourist resource and was apparently fairly bustling. At present, Subaru and the others were in the position of waiting for the area to be cleared of people.


"Before that, nobody even glanced at it just because there was a big tree there, but now, honestly, everyone's so fickle it's ridiculous."


The one who called out with that was Ferris, who was just as idle as they were.

Leaning his back against the dragon carriage, he lined up beside Subaru and the others as they gazed at the remains of the Great Tree, narrowing his yellow eyes and grumbling his thoughts about the circumstances surrounding the tree. Subaru agreed with him on that point. Even though evening had already passed and night was approaching from beyond the sky, the flow of people showed no sign of stopping.

The stump business could be called an unexpected byproduct of the White Whale subjugation.


"Even so, I'm surprised that I'd end up coming to Flügel's Great Tree with Subaru-kun again."


"I didn't think I'd have another role to play here either. Back then, when I heard this spot was treated as a marker for the 'Witch,' I proposed a different plan during the White Whale thing too."


"Oh? A different plan, like what?"


"Like, squeeze out power rivaling cutting down the Great Tree with everyone's wisdom and courage."


"Ahaha, amazing. Subaru-kun is such a geni-us."


Being told that in a tone that clearly didn't mean it, Subaru gave a wry smile and glanced sideways at the other person.

Ferris had been acting together with them for two months already, but Subaru still wasn't used to seeing him in male attire. In terms of time alone, he had spent far longer with him than when Ferris was dressed as a woman.

He didn't think the reason was simply that the female appearance had suited him so well. —The reason this lingering discomfort remained was something Subaru couldn't quite put into words himself.


"Ferris, are you really coming along too?"


"Hmm~, does Beatrice-chan not want me to?"


"It's not about want or not. Your power is valuable. If something happened to you..."


"You're worried about me? How sweet. But no need to worry."


Ferris poked the nose of Beatrice—who was being held by Subaru—with his finger. At that, Beatrice's eyes went round, and Ferris softened the corners of his eyes slightly,


"The more uncertain the place we're going, the more you want me there. If it's me, even if your head flies off, I can save you as long as it's within one second."


"...Betty is a spirit, so ordinary healing magic doesn't work well on her."


"Yeah, I know. So over these two months, I made sure to update myself."


"—You're serious."


"Serious serious. —In front of me, even people who want to die won't be allowed to die, you know."


Perhaps that was truly an extraordinary claim, because Beatrice, upon hearing Ferris's reply, made a small sound in her throat as if her mouth had gone dry. But from Subaru's perspective, it was incredibly reassuring.

In actual fact, ever since the battle against the "Beloved Child" began, a tension like being watched by a blade at all hours had been crackling in the air.


"Also, I've been meaning to say this for a while..."




"—Ferris?"


Ferris, who had brought it up himself, fell silent, and Subaru tilted his head. For a while after that, Ferris opened and closed his lips repeatedly, but—


"—You don't need to worry yourself over every little thing about me and Crusch-sama."


"Ugh, you noticed."


"Of course it was obvious. ...I do feel sorry for making you worry, though."


"Though?"


"Subaru-kun, if unrelated people started saying all sorts of things about you and Emilia-sama, you'd get upset too, right? I think it's the same thing."


To that analogy, Subaru felt that Ferris was by no means an "unrelated person," but since that probably wasn't the point of the conversation, he held back his objection.

That said, if there were something he could say, he would like to—


"...The stump looks like there's movement. I'll go get ready."


But mercilessly, just when he wanted to say something, Ferris noticed movement on the stump side and turned his back. He then hurried back to the dragon carriage, and the reason became clear immediately. Crusch was coming over from the direction of the stump.


"Honestly, that guy..."


"—? Preparations on this side are complete. Did something happen, 'Ash Star'?"


"Nah, nothing, Crusch-san... ah, wait, my bad. 'Verdant Beauty,' 'Verdant Beauty.'"


Flustered that the code name had slipped out when he let his guard down, Subaru hastily covered his mouth in self-reproach. At that, Crusch relaxed her lips slightly and continued, "It's fine,"


"As long as we are not in a place with listening ears, I will answer to either name. I am quite fond of the ring of 'Verdant Beauty' that you gave me as well."


"Well, if the person herself likes it, then I'm glad it wasn't overstepping. At least you didn't get stuck with something that's practically an insult, like 'Long Tongue.'"


"Insult..."


"It's one of Subaru's mysterious obsessions. It's a disease he sometimes has, so you don't need to worry about it. More importantly, the clearing of people was also the work of 'White Sheep,' I take it."


"Yes, that's right. As always, 'White Sheep's' negotiation is splendid. I don't sense the sort of craftiness that comes from verbal manipulation... but there's something about that person that naturally makes one want to listen."


"It's the virtue only granted to fragile, beautiful girls, I guess."


Thinking back, there were quite a number of beautiful people just in Subaru's vicinity, but starting with Emilia, there might be almost no one who suited the expression "fragile" in the typical sense.


"Thinking about it, everyone seems like beauty overflowing with vitality. Wait, could it be that the most mopey and fragile one here is... me...?"


"Hmm, I do not understand the cause of your unease, but if anything ever happens, you may rely on me at any time. As long as it is within my power, I will wield this sword without hesitation."


"A powerful beauty, right off the bat...! If I were the heroine, this would be dangerous... ow ow ow."


"Honestly, playing around again like that."


As Subaru's heart fluttered at Crusch's reliability, Beatrice pinched his cheek as punishment. Crusch smiled at the sight of them, then turned back toward the stump.

And then—


"—To think that you and I would visit Flügel's Great Tree again."


""Ah.""


"—? What is it? Did I say something strange?"


Subaru and Beatrice, who had let out surprised voices in unison, quickly shook their heads side by side at Crusch's uneasy look, saying, "No, no,"


"It's not that. It's just that, right now, like just now,"


"Ferris said the exact same thing as you just now."


"That fellow, the same thing as me... I see. That makes sense."


Her raised eyebrows lasted only a moment before Crusch nodded with an expression of understanding.

It was only natural—if Subaru, Crusch, and Ferris came to this place together, what came to mind for all of them was the White Whale subjugation battle. —Wilhelm, who had been the protagonist of that battle, was also one of those who had gone missing from the royal capital, his whereabouts unknown.


"I heard Wilhelm-san is together with our people. It's worrying, but..."


"No, if it's Wilhelm, he will overcome most difficulties. If we're going to worry, Father is far more concerning. Being the worrier that he is, he must be under considerable stress."


"—"


"However, Father was also a duke before he passed the headship to me. In times of national crisis, he will hold his ground with body and soul. What we can do is bring this situation to a conclusion as soon as possible."


"I agree. That's what today's plan is for."


Crusch, trusting in the core strength of her separated vassals and family, nodded deeply at Beatrice's words. Subaru also nodded back, feeling his resolve tighten anew.


"Don't worry. After all, this place is a lucky spot for us. The operations we've run at Flügel's Great Tree have never once failed."


"That is... fufu, that's certainly true."


At Subaru's lighthearted remark with shrugged shoulders, Crusch smiled with the tension leaving her face. Winking at that smile, Subaru vowed to himself that he would not let that lighthearted remark remain mere lightheartedness.

Subaru and the others worried about their companions just the same. If the solution to that was saving the kingdom, then they needed to put the king in check as soon as possible.

For that purpose—


"—Let's go, Beako's homecoming visit!"


"That way of saying it gives Betty a vague, indescribable itchiness!"



　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　△▼△▼△▼△



"—For my part, the first priority is being able to verify the authenticity of the information obtained. Making contact with Roswaal can be postponed. However..."


"However?"


"If, when the time comes that you want to rely on that knowledge, another alternative means appears, then whether there is any significance in maintaining a friendly relationship with Roswaal becomes a matter for debate."


"Well, you know, friendship isn't about expecting something in return..."


At Subaru's unconvincing follow-up, Russell made a clearly sour face.

Subaru would like to think that human relationships aren't built solely on calculations of merits and demerits, but it was difficult to measure the values of the "Six Tongues" chancellor by common sense.


"And besides, the other party is Roswaal... If anything, he's the one whose criteria for viewing people are incomprehensible, so I don't think he's in any position to complain about how others see him."


Subaru thought that, despite appearances, Roswaal's human side was part of his charm, but the problem lay in how he would brazenly trample over that human side with his ironclad sense of purpose.

Keeping the friendship issue between Roswaal and Russell in the corner of his mind—


"But, is this okay? We've been avoiding conspicuous actions as much as possible to not be found by the enemy, and now we're clearing people out of a tourist attraction."


"Of course, it is not a desirable situation. No matter how much effort is expended, rumors seep out like raindrops. So it will likely be a one-night contest."


"One night, huh."


Looking up at the night sky, Subaru clenched his fist at the precariousness of the limited time.

Right now, Beatrice was circling the area around the Great Tree's stump, searching for the port she sought—a kind of hole in space connected to the castle through the effect of "Door Crossing." But according to her, it had been several hundred years since she last left the castle, and the environment around the port should have drastically changed.


"Even though Beako's the only one who can find the key, it's frustrating that I can only leave it to her. Please, don't rush. But also, hurry without panicking..."


"HEY! What are you spacing out for! She says it's been found!"


"Seriously!?"


Just as Subaru was praying, Rachins's cheer rang out, and Subaru exchanged glances with Russell before rushing over to where Beatrice had been contending with the stump.

Even from a distance, the stump had looked like a small hill, but up close, its extraordinary size became even more striking, making it understandable why it had become a tourist attraction.


"And having little Beako next to such a stump makes it look like a scene out of a fairy tale."


"You flatter me. Well, it's true that Betty is a mischievous little imp like the Tinker Bell from the fairy tales Subaru told Betty."


"Little imp? I've only ever thought of you as a smart, cute, super-competent great spirit. So? So? So?"


"Honestly, you're too excited! Now, all of you come over here too."


Though she must have been relieved to have found what she was looking for, Beatrice beckoned them over with a smug expression that hid it well, and the group jogged over to gather around her.

"Impressive." "That's amazing!" "So it wasn't just talk and attitude." "...That's amazing," they praised her in turns, and Beatrice, in a good mood, cleared her throat with an "Ahem,"


"Honestly, since there was a possibility that the port had been set with the Great Tree as its base point, Betty thought the odds were fifty-fifty. But it's properly here. It's just sleeping."


"Sleeping, you say. In that case, is it possible to wake it?"


"Naturally. —Betty is Mother's greatest masterpiece, after all."


With a grin, Beatrice extended her small hand toward Subaru.

Accepting that as a matter of course, Subaru took her hand, lifted her light body up in one motion, and stood together with her where she had been standing.

And then—


"Subaru, Betty needs to borrow your power for the 'Door Crossing.'"


"Don't be ridiculous. Ever since I brought you out, I've been the one constantly borrowing your power!"


"—Shall we?"


Through their joined hands and touching bodies, Subaru's mana flowed into Beatrice. —Apparently, it was quite a heavy sleeper, and he could feel his stored mana being consumed all at once.

He'd really prefer it to wake up before Subaru withered and ran dry, but—


"...Mana depletion isn't something I can help with, so don't die, okay."


"Don't jinx it...!"


Perhaps feeling uneasy at Subaru's rapidly decreasing mana, Ferris muttered that quietly, and Subaru snapped back. —It was right after that.

Suddenly, the stump pulsed. —No, there was a reaction in the space just before it.


"—"


It wasn't that there was an actual sound.

But everyone present likely felt a great wave of mana from beyond the gate.

Soon, a single line of pale purple light ran through the reacting space, slowly tracing through the air, and began drawing a picture of light in that spot.


"This is..."


"—I see. A door."


Crusch voiced the conclusion after her astonishment, and just as she said, the purple light that had started from Beatrice's raised hand traced through the air and created a single door of light—a port.

It was, without any room for doubt, a perfect entrance to a hidden dungeon.


"What is its condition?"


"It's stable. It just had trouble waking up; once woken, it should last for a while. Even if Betty lets go... like this."


Matching Beatrice as she withdrew her hand, Subaru also stepped back. Even so, the port that had appeared before them showed no change, nor did it vanish from beneath their feet.

Seeing that, Russell nodded with a "Very good," and—


"—'Black Dog.'"


"Yeah yeah, I know already. Dangerous and risky jobs are best left to someone whose chest wouldn't ache even if he died—right!"


"It is simply a matter of the right person in the right place. You are also a piece that would hurt to lose, without a doubt."


"Kahaha! I'm so happy and so miserable I could cry-y-y!"


Waving his hand flippantly, 'Black Dog' stepped forward while throwing out sarcasm. He reached his hand toward the port and, without hesitation, tried to open it.

From behind him, 'White Sheep' called out with a nervous, high-pitched voice, "'Black Dog,'"


"P-please be careful."


"Good grief, every single one of you... This is the little missy's homecoming after who-knows-how-many years, decades, centuries, right? Don't badmouth her family home so much!"


Even though it was a retort, unlike the one directed at Russell, his words to 'White Sheep' seemed to carry a shade of consideration for her. Subaru didn't fully understand the relationship between these two either, but it was strange—despite all their back-and-forth, they seemed to trust each other.

At any rate—


"—"


Before everyone's eyes, 'Black Dog' touched the port—and with a slick motion, his figure was swallowed into the other side of the space. Truly, there was an entrance to an invisible space in midair.

'Black Dog's' body did not pass through to the other side of the port; rather, he arrived somewhere connected across the separated space. Then, before Subaru and the others who were holding their breath, some time passed—and slowly, only his hand returned to this side, raising his thumb firmly.


"That's..."


"Ah, it seems to be a sign for 'all clear.' It's a gesture from my hometown."


"Recently, 'Ash Star' has been getting along well with 'Black Dog,' hasn't he?"


"Getting along... is that getting along? Well, knowing that he's someone who won't bite is progress, I guess."


Though he hadn't intended it, the fact that he was sending signs with his fingers made Subaru feel now, belatedly, that 'Black Dog' was being treated like the dog in his code name. A large dog that wouldn't warm up to someone—he was far too dangerous to ever place near Beatrice.

In any case—


"If there are no problems, then proceed. —'White Sheep,' you next."


"Y-yes! Um... here I go."


Prompted by Russell, 'White Sheep' hesitated slightly but timidly took hold of 'Black Dog's' hand, which was still extended to this side. Immediately, her hand was tugged forcefully, and with a "Eek!" of surprise, her figure also vanished beyond the port.


"Tch, why the hell do I have to go into a castle connected to a 'Witch'..."


"Stop grumbling, 'Long Tongue.' Your quick wits are dependable. Felt has found a fine servant."


"Shut up! I'm not a damn servant! I'm just a tagalong by circumstance, a tagalong!"


And so, after that, Rachins—who had apparently ended up accompanying the Royal Selection by circumstance—and Crusch, who seemed utterly fearless, boarded the port. Just before following them, Ferris glanced toward Subaru, likely worried about his earlier mana depletion.


"I'm fine, full of energy. Honestly, you look light enough that I could lift you too."


"No thank you! ...If anything happens, tell me right away, okay?"


Leaving only those words behind, Ferris also went beyond the port. In Subaru's arms as he watched him go, Beatrice stirred and,


"He said that."


"He sure did. —So, Russell-san, are you going too?"


Watching the group pass through the port one after another, Subaru looked at Russell, who remained last.

As the chancellor of the "Six Tongues" and someone in an irreplaceable position, Russell surely couldn't just go directly to the field himself—or so Subaru had thought when asking, but unexpectedly, Russell shook his head and,


"If possible, I would like to devote myself to a directing role as well, but when it comes to investigating the 'Witch's' research, there is no reason not to use these eyes of mine."


"Ah, the 'Divine Protection of Appraisal.'"


"Indeed. When it comes to the 'Witch's' research, one must be prepared for things beyond the understanding of ordinary people. However, even for things I cannot understand, these eyes can discern the presence and degree of their value."


"I see... When you put it that way, it's certainly an indispensable skill for a dungeon crawl."


In games like hack-and-slash dungeon crawlers, it was common to bring back unidentified items and not know whether they were valuable until examining them on the surface, but Russell's Divine Protection could at least determine the value of those items.

Being spared from grabbing things at random and ending up with nothing but wasted effort was very much appreciated.


"Therefore, I will leave securing this location to other personnel and accompany you."


So saying, Russell snapped his fingers and sent a complex signal with his hands toward the dragon carriage.

It seemed to be a code unique to the "Six Tongues," and since Subaru occasionally saw it being used, he had been trying to learn it as appropriate, but it wasn't going well.


"It would be troublesome if it could be easily learned, so that is correct. Though it is equally troublesome to have someone like 'Black Dog' who has no intention of learning."


"'Black Dog' seemed to take a liking to Subaru's hand signs too, so it might be a problem of how it's taught. Or maybe a problem of who's teaching."


"What a nuisance. —Well then, I shall go on ahead."


As if plugging his ears to inconvenient topics, Russell passed through the port first and crossed over to the other side.

With that, the only ones left before the stump were Subaru and Beatrice. They could immediately chase after the backs of those who had gone ahead, but—


"—I can't wait four hundred years, but shall we wait about three hundred until you've sorted out your feelings?"


"...Honestly, you're being too considerate. Betty's nervousness is just a tiny bit."


Beatrice, in his arms, relaxed her slightly stiffened body and smiled softly. Then, gazing at the entrance of the home she hadn't returned to in four hundred years,


"—Now!"


"Right on!"


With pointless vigor, Subaru, holding Beatrice, hopped into the port.

In that instant, there was a faint sensation of passing through a thin curtain of light, and the grassy air of the Lifaus Plains receded behind them—instead, a cold, dry wind greeted the two.


"—"


The soles of his feet landed on the sensation of a stone floor.

Directly behind them, the port connecting to the Great Tree's remains was open just as it had been on the entrance side, and the six who had entered before were standing a short distance away, looking around.

It was a dark place with no light source. The only reason the figures of their companions who had entered first were visible was that 'Black Dog' and Rachins had both lit flames at their fingertips as makeshift lights.

Was this a castle? The air itself seemed to carry a unique heaviness, though whether that was because it was the 'Witch's' house or the weight of four hundred years of silence, he couldn't tell.


"So this is..."


"Kugelschreiber Castle."


Subaru, murmuring nervously, gently lowered Beatrice—who was tapping his shoulder—to the floor.

Beatrice walked, stepping on the castle floor with her own feet. The sound of her shoes echoed through the castle interior, carrying the reverberation far through the walls and pillars buried in darkness and the long corridors extending left and right.

As if welcoming the return of a resident after four hundred years.


"—"


Closing her eyes, Beatrice took in the castle's air with her small body.

While grateful to the others for staying silent out of consideration for Beatrice's feelings, Subaru placed his hand lightly on Beatrice's head in place of the soundless castle.

And then—


"Welcome home, Beako."


"—. I'm home."


As if responding to that greeting, the castle walls suddenly began to glow faintly.

Subaru, holding back the others who instinctively tensed up with a gesture, looked around and recognized that faint light—he remembered the tomb in the "Sanctuary" glowing the same way inside.

Indeed, for a castle that claimed to belong to the "Witch of Greed," there seemed to be an extraordinary number of commonalities.


The castle. —It was, unmistakably, a castle.

However—


"Even so... it's really fallen into ruin."


The light drove back the darkness, illuminating the castle's interior more clearly than moonlight. —The interior that became visible was, to put it kindly, not in a state fit for receiving guests.

After all, the walls were caved in as if crushed by a giant palm, and some of the thick pillars had been forcibly bent from their midpoints. The ceiling was heavily gouged, the shattered stone fragments compressed together and, inexplicably, rolled into spheres. The corridor continuing deeper into the castle was collapsed halfway on one side, and the doors, windows, even the floor could not maintain their original forms.


"This isn't the kind of ruin that comes from being left alone for a long time. Unless someone was extremely bad at cleaning up, is this why Beako left the castle with her mother?"


"That's right. Mother wasn't very good at tidying up, but this is different from that. —It's the work of the 'Demon of Melancholy.'"


"The Demon of Melancholy... that's, if I remember right..."


It was a name he had heard before from Lewes Meyer, the administrator of the "Sanctuary."

The barrier that had existed in the "Sanctuary," the trigger for its creation, and the cause of Beatrice's parting with her first friend. —A hidden extra sin, concealed from history.


"Subaru heard about it from Lewes too. —The 'Demon of Melancholy' Hector was a long-time enemy of Betty, Mother, and, as an aside, Roswaal. He was obsessed with Mother and followed her around endlessly."


"Followed her around... A stalker of the 'Witch'—what an outrageous person existed. Did he fall that hard for your mother?"


"In terms of warped affection, perhaps. But it wasn't about love or infatuation. After all, Hector was Mother's father."


"—"


At those words, Subaru gasped at the unexpected revelation. Instinctively, the fingers he had moved to his chest touched the black sphere hanging from his neck, and he gripped it for some reason.

—The 'Demon of Melancholy' Hector, the father of the 'Witch of Greed' Echidna.


"The more information I get, the more that Hector sounds like an absolute bastard. ...Ah, but even when I say Echidna, it's the Echidna on Beako's side, so that's fine...? No, damn it, this is confusing. Shit, Echidna...!"


"No need to get so flustered. Don't worry, Hector... Mother's father is no longer anywhere. Not just the father—the mother too."


"With that phrasing, the mother sounds like she has quite a story too..."


"She was a 'Witch.' —The 'Witch of Vanity' Pandora. That was Mother's mother."


Saying it as if it were nothing, Beatrice began walking with small steps.

Honestly, Subaru was considerably shocked by this newly received information.


"Father was 'Melancholy,' and mother was 'Vanity'..."


Both were human sins that had been included among the old Deadly Sins before the seven took their current form.

He had heard that there were those who bore those past sins, but for both of them to be connected to Echidna—if she was the child of a 'Witch' and a 'Demon,' then it made sense, in a way.

Thinking back, Echidna had seemed particularly fixated on the concept of 'Witches' at the tea parties. Whether that was connected to her origins, he couldn't say for certain—


"...I really should have talked with you more."


Regretting an opportunity that would never come again, Subaru muttered that.


Leaving Subaru to his thoughts, Beatrice walked ahead, and there, a tremendously large hole had opened in the floor, which 'White Sheep' was timidly peering into.


"'White Sheep,' be careful not to accidentally fall in while peeking—that wouldn't be funny."


Standing beside 'White Sheep,' Beatrice kicked a stone fragment from the floor into the hole. —However, no sound of the fragment hitting the bottom could be heard.

While not only 'White Sheep' but everyone else's eyes went wide at that fact, Beatrice placed her hands on her hips and turned back to the group,


"This is Kugelschreiber Castle... the 'Witch's' house, floating in the sky of a different space. If you fall, you will absolutely never come back, so everyone must walk with care."


And with that, she gave a lecture on how to walk in her family home of four hundred years.
